NAME
       systemd-run - run a command as a temporary systemd unit

SYNOPSIS
       systemd-run [--unit=NAME] [--on-calendar=WHEN] [--user] COMMAND...

DESCRIPTION
       systemd-run starts COMMAND in the background as a transient service: a
       unit that exists only while it runs and needs no unit file. Its output
       goes to the journal and it can be watched with systemctl status. With
       a timer option it creates a transient timer instead, which runs the
       command later. Nothing created this way survives a reboot.

OPTIONS
       --unit=NAME
              The unit's name (otherwise one like run-u123.service is made up).

       --on-calendar=WHEN, --on-active=TIME
              Run the command at a calendar time, or after a delay.

       --user Use the user's own systemd.

EXAMPLES
           systemd-run --unit=cleanup rm -rf /tmp/oldfiles
           systemctl status cleanup

SEE ALSO
       systemctl(1), systemd.timer(5), at(1)
